            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### Limiting Reactant (The One That Runs Out First)",
            "start": 2258,
            "end": 2309
          },
          {
            "id": "p38",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Sometimes you don't have \"plenty\" of everything. The reactant that gets used up first limits how much product you can make.",
            "start": 2310,
            "end": 2433
          },
          {
            "id": "p39",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Example: If you have 4 moles of H₂ but only 1 mole of O₂, oxygen is the limiting reactant (because the equation needs 2 H₂ per 1 O₂, so you can only use 2 moles of H₂). You'll only make 2 moles of water, and you'll have leftover hydrogen.",
            "start": 2435,
            "end": 2673
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The definition is accurate and the illustrative problem (4 moles H₂ and 1 mole O₂ producing 2 moles H₂O with oxygen limiting) is chemically sound and correctly solved.",
          "errors": []
        }
